In crates/chronica-api/src/routines/mod.rs, the function `has_tasks_assign` (lines 39-47) authorizes the `tasks:assign` permission by reading the RAW `x-chronica-permissions` HTTP header off the request `HeaderMap`:

    pub(super) fn has_tasks_assign(h: &HeaderMap, actor: &Principal) -> bool {
        if actor.is_board() {
            return true;
        }
        h.get("x-chronica-permissions")
            .and_then(|v| v.to_str().ok())
            .map(|s| s.split(',').any(|p| p.trim() == "tasks:assign"))
            .unwrap_or(false)
    }

This defeats the Phase 3C signed-principal trust model. In signed (production) mode the outermost `principal_layer` (src/router.rs:541, src/principal_auth.rs:378) verifies a signed EdDSA-JWT principal and stamps a `VerifiedPrincipal` (which carries a verified `permissions: Vec<String>` field, populated from token claims at src/principal_auth.rs:323) into request extensions — but it does NOT strip the raw `x-chronica-permissions` header. So an attacker presenting a valid signed token WITHOUT `tasks:assign` in its claims, while ALSO sending a forged raw header `x-chronica-permissions: tasks:assign`, passes the gate. This is a privilege escalation on every routine mutation: create (routines_crud.rs:51), patch assignee/status (routines_crud.rs:168), run (runs.rs:82), restore (revisions.rs:80), and the 3 trigger ops (triggers.rs:38/143/247).

Fix: `has_tasks_assign` must read from the verified principal, not the raw header. Change it to check `actor.is_board() || actor.permissions.iter().any(|p| p == "tasks:assign")` and drop the `HeaderMap` argument (or ignore it). Update all 6 call sites accordingly. Then add a regression test in crates/chronica-api/tests/routines_route.rs using `raw_request_with_headers` (from tests/common/mod.rs) that sends a valid signed token for a permless non-board user PLUS a raw `x-chronica-permissions: tasks:assign` header and asserts 403 — i.e. the raw header must NOT escalate. Note: the existing routine tests authorize via `x-chronica-actor-type: board`, so they do not exercise the permissions path at all; the new negative test is the one that locks the fix.

Verify with: DATABASE_URL=... cargo test -p chronica-api --features db --test routines_route

