1	//! Dev-only signed-principal issuer — the auth bridge, Option 1 (judge panel `wf_3defec57`, recorded
2	//! in `docs/design/production-readiness.md`).
3	//!
4	//! # Why
5	//! Lets the UI run AUTHENTICATED locally by MINTING a short-lived signed principal token with a DEV
6	//! key, then sending it as `Authorization: Bearer` to the UNCHANGED signed verifier
7	//! ([`crate::principal_auth`]). This exercises real `PrincipalAuthMode::Signed` verification locally —
8	//! NOT the `CHRONICA_TRUST_HEADERS` bypass — so local runs behave like production minus the issuer.
9	//!
10	//! # The two CODE linchpins (a leaked minter = worse-than-F1 total auth bypass — config drift, not crypto)
11	//! * **L1 — DISJOINT KEY.** The dev signing key is NOT a member of any production JWKS, so a dev-minted
12	//!   token presented to a production verifier fails (`UnknownKid` / `BadSignature` ⇒ reject). The
13	//!   security model is this disjointness + L2, NOT secrecy of the dev seed. Proven by
14	//!   `dev_token_is_rejected_by_a_foreign_jwks`.
15	//! * **L2 — DEV-GATED / PROD-REFUSED.** Enabled ONLY by `CHRONICA_DEV_AUTH=1|true`; [`DevAuthConfig::from_env`]
16	//!   returns `Err` when it is set in production so boot ABORTS (mirrors the `TrustHeaders`-in-production
17	//!   guard at `lib.rs:174-185`). The `/api/dev/login` route is mounted ONLY when this is `Some`.
18	//!
19	//! Token MINTING stays dev-only. The PRODUCTION answer is an external IdP / gateway (escalated to a
20	//! human): its public JWKS drops into `CHRONICA_PRINCIPAL_JWKS` with ZERO verifier change.
